Verdict: challenge. The bytes verdict and unchanged move cases work, but the promised CHANGED-since answer is not complete.

Counterexample: accept X=1; commit X=2; branch side and make an unrelated commit; on master restore X=1;
merge side with --no-ff --no-commit, set X=2, and commit. The restored commit reads UNCHANGED. At the merge,
drift reads CHANGED since the original acceptance commit, although that commit held the accepted bytes.
The first_change candidate logs simplify history: both the merge and restoration are omitted; first_change
returns None and compare falls back to the acceptance commit. This is not merely a deterministic choice
between parallel changes: the returned commit did not change the bytes at all.

Reproduced end to end through drift_repo/gated and drift.lines in the named commit X (an unmodified git archive)
and current base M. Base location: warrant/pins.py:69-80,99-102; X location: warrant/drift.py:first_change/compare.
A scratch in-memory control that adds --full-history -m to the candidate logs makes the same case pass at M.
This control is diagnostic, not a reviewed or delivered fix. No repository code or regression test was changed.

Reproduction: save the Python below as merge_probe.py outside the checkout, then run
TMPDIR=<scratch-directory> python3 -B merge_probe.py <checkout-at-M-or-X>
It prints FAIL G14-MERGE-SINCE on the reviewed implementations. Fixture signer keys are deterministic test keys,
not operator checker seeds; no production checker seed was read and no production replay was signed.

Environment: Python 3.15.2; git version 2.54.0 (Apple Git-157).
Checked at M: due --actor codex --json matches D/T; MCP context waits only on codex's roadmap/acceptor;
PR #299 is merged at X; tools_test.py 99 cases passed; roadmap check 87 lineages, 0 problems;
claims check 118 live claims, 0 problems. Eight targeted mutants killed (matrix below).
Fresh additional probes: merge-only edit passed; moved regular file becoming a symlink passed;
